<BaseLayout title="SIGWAY primordial-spectrum lab" description="Paint a primordial curvature spectrum and explore its scalar-induced gravitational-wave response."><header class="page-head"><div class="shell"><span class="eyebrow">Interactive lab · SIGWAY</span><h1>From primordial structure to gravitational waves</h1><p>Choose a template or paint your own binned primordial curvature spectrum, then follow how SIGWAY maps it into an induced gravitational-wave signal.</p></div></header><section class="lab-stage"><div class="shell"><SigwayExplorer/></div></section><div class="prose lab-notes"><h2>What is being calculated?</h2><p>Scalar perturbations generate gravitational waves at second order when they re-enter the horizon. The signal at a given frequency is sourced by pairs of curvature modes, making the map from the primordial spectrum to the gravitational-wave spectrum quadratic and non-local: a narrow primordial feature can produce a broader, structured response.</p><p>The presets illustrate several qualitatively different primordial shapes rather than fitted spectra. The 50-bin view is the standard resolution; 100 bins allow finer features and make it easier to see which details survive the broad mode-coupling kernel. Detector overlays provide the observational scale for LISA, PTAs, and LIGO.</p></div></BaseLayout>
<style is:global>.lab-stage{background:var(--paper-deep);margin-top:clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.75rem);padding-block:clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2.5rem)}.lab-notes{margin-top:clamp(2.5rem,4vw,3.5rem)}</style>
